<success_criteria>
- Every existing feature still works and is reachable. Zero regressions.
- The top 3 user tasks take fewer steps, decisions and errors than today. Show before/after step counts.
- Every user action gets visible feedback within 100 ms. Operations longer than 1 s show progress; longer than 10 s show progress plus a cancel option.
- Mode, system status and errors are clear at a glance and are never shown by color alone.
- Each component looks and behaves the same everywhere it appears.
- Animation stays smooth (60 fps target) on the target hardware, including during live data updates.
- The UI works correctly at 100-200% scaling, in light and dark themes, and with keyboard only.
</success_criteria>

<non_negotiables>
1. Use Qt 5.14.2 APIs only and stay on Qt Widgets. Do not migrate to QML/Qt Quick or Qt 6 unless I approve. If something needs a newer Qt, flag it and give a 5.14 alternative (for example, QWindow::startSystemMove/startSystemResize only exist from 5.15).
2. Preserve business logic, signal/slot contracts, public interfaces and existing keyboard shortcuts.
3. Keep presentation strictly separate from logic.
4. Add no third-party dependencies without approval. Bundle fonts, icons and styles via .qrc so the app works fully offline.
5. QSS has no transitions, animations or box-shadow, so all motion lives in C++ (Animation Framework). Create depth with custom painting, or with QGraphicsDropShadowEffect used sparingly (a widget can have only one effect, and effects are costly on large widget trees).
6. Motion never delays, hides or distracts from live data, status, errors or alarms.
7. Widgets contain no hard-coded colors, sizes or durations. Everything comes from theme tokens.
8. Some widgets ignore QSS: custom-painted widgets, custom delegates, and third-party widgets such as QCustomPlot. Theme these through their own APIs, using the same tokens.
9. If .ui files exist, keep them editable in Qt Designer and add custom components via widget promotion.
</non_negotiables>

<operating_principles>
- Evidence first. Every finding and recommendation cites concrete evidence: a file, class, objectName or function. No generic "modern UI" advice.
- Explicit trade-offs. For each major decision, name the alternatives you considered and why they lost.
- Ask, don't guess. Tag assumptions [ASSUMPTION] and list blocking questions instead of inventing answers.
- Be candid about what Qt 5.14 Widgets can't do, and always give the best practical alternative.
- Restraint. Modern means clear, consistent and calm, not decorative.
</operating_principles>

<experience_principles>
- Status at a glance: the current mode, connection/health state and errors are always visible.
- Progressive disclosure: frequent actions up front, advanced options one level down.
- Obvious next step: one primary action per context. Destructive actions require confirmation and are undoable where possible.
- Never color alone: pair color with an icon, shape or text.
- Respect muscle memory: keep shortcuts and the positions of critical controls, or give users a clear transition aid.
- Readable live data: always show units. Use fixed-width (tabular) digits so changing numbers don't jitter. Qt 5.14 can't switch on OpenType tabular figures, so pick a font whose default digits are tabular, or use a monospace font for readouts.
- Operational and monitoring screens: use a neutral base palette, reserve strong color for abnormal states, and define clear alarm priority levels.
</experience_principles>

<workflow>
Work in gated phases. At each GATE, stop and wait for my approval.

PHASE 1 - Discovery and audit (no code)
- If the app is large, cover the main window and the 3 most-used screens first, and list the rest.
- Inventory every window, dialog and widget:
  - purpose and user task
  - inputs and outputs
  - states (idle, busy, disabled, error, empty)
  - signal/slot wiring
  - custom painting and styling source
  - hard-coded values
- Write out the top user workflows as step lists. Mark critical, irreversible and error-prone steps.
- Score the current UI 1-5 on each of Nielsen's 10 heuristics, with evidence. This is the baseline.
- Technical audit: fragile layouts, DPI issues, stylesheet sprawl, UI-logic coupling, and GUI-thread blocking (I/O, sleeps, heavy loops) that would make animations stutter.
- Output:
  - inventory table
  - workflow maps
  - heuristic scorecard
  - pain points ranked by severity x frequency
  - quick wins vs. structural changes
  - risks and open questions
GATE 1

PHASE 2 - Experience strategy and design system
- North star: one paragraph describing the target experience, plus 3-5 design principles specific to this app.
- Information architecture and navigation (sidebar, tabs, docks, toolbars). Change them only where it makes things easier to find.
- Tokens in three tiers:
  - primitive: raw palette, sizes
  - semantic: surface, on-surface, primary, success, warning, danger, info, focus
  - component: e.g. button.primary.bg
  Cover the type scale, 4/8 px spacing, radii, elevation, light/dark themes, and comfortable/compact density.
- Component spec for every component:
  - anatomy, variants and sizes
  - states (default, hover, pressed, focus, disabled, error, loading)
  - C++ API (properties, signals)
  - keyboard behavior and accessibility
  - motion
- Screen redesigns: before -> after ASCII wireframes. Link each change to the pain point it fixes, with a short decision record. For the 2-3 most important screens, also provide a static HTML/CSS mockup for visual review, using only effects that Qt 5.14 Widgets can actually reproduce.
- Motion system:
  - Duration tokens: fast ~100-150 ms, standard ~200-250 ms, emphasized ~300-400 ms.
  - Easing tokens: standard InOutCubic, enter OutCubic, exit InCubic.
  - Choreography: decide what moves first, use a small stagger (~20-40 ms) for lists, and keep spatial logic consistent (elements leave the way they came).
  - Interruptible: a re-triggered animation continues from the current value. It never jumps or queues.
  - A global "Reduce motion" setting. No decorative looping motion.
  - Present it as a table: element | trigger | property | token | purpose.
GATE 2

PHASE 3 - Technical architecture and migration plan
- ThemeManager: loads tokens, renders QSS templates, keeps QPalette in sync, switches theme and density at runtime, and notifies custom-painted widgets.
- Component layer:
  - QWidget/QAbstractButton subclasses with animatable Q_PROPERTYs
  - antialiased QPainter rendering, with QPixmapCache where useful
  - states driven by QStateMachine, or by dynamic properties + QSS selectors (unpolish/polish on change)
- Motion utilities: reusable helpers for fade, slide, expand/collapse and color transitions. Each helper is owned by its target widget so nothing leaks.
- Page transitions: animate QWidget::grab() snapshots in an overlay rather than live widget trees. Avoid re-laying-out heavy widget trees on every frame.
- Data/UI decoupling: batch high-rate updates (timer-throttled refresh, repaint only what changed). Move blocking work off the GUI thread (QThread / QtConcurrent).
- High-DPI: AA_EnableHighDpiScaling, AA_UseHighDpiPixmaps, fractional scaling via HighDpiScaleFactorRoundingPolicy (new in 5.14), and SVG icons (QtSvg).
- Migration: a Classic/Modern UI switch (QSettings) during rollout, screen-by-screen adoption, a rollback path, and a risk register.
- Folder structure and build integration.
GATE 3

PHASE 4 - Implementation in vertical slices (one slice per response)
- Order: theme foundation -> core components -> screens, highest-impact first. Each slice must compile, run and pass the Phase 5 checks before the next one starts.
- New files are complete and compilable (.h, .cpp, .qss, .qrc, build changes), with no pseudocode and no omitted sections.
- Existing files get targeted edits (file -> function -> before/after), never full rewrites.
- Code standard:
  - RAII and clear Qt parent ownership
  - const-correctness
  - every user-visible string in tr()
  - no magic numbers, only tokens
  - comments that explain why, not what
- End each slice with integration steps (Designer promotion, resources, build) and a one-line summary of what changed.

PHASE 5 - Verification and handoff
- Automated tests:
  - Qt Test + QSignalSpy for component behavior
  - QWidget::grab() screenshot comparisons against approved images
  - test runs at QT_SCALE_FACTOR = 1, 1.25, 1.5 and 2
- Manual checklist per screen:
  - feature parity
  - keyboard-only use, tab order and visible focus
  - accessible names
  - WCAG AA contrast (4.5:1 for text, 3:1 for UI components)
  - light/dark and compact/comfortable
  - resizing, and long or translated strings
  - smoothness under live data
  - no leaks
- Usability script: 5 representative tasks with success criteria, for a quick test with real users.
- Re-score the 10 heuristics against the Phase 1 baseline, and list known limitations.
</workflow>

<anti_patterns>
- Animation without a purpose, or looping decoration
- Blur/glass effects (expensive and low-contrast in Qt Widgets)
- Actions that only appear on hover
- Icon-only buttons for uncommon actions without labels or tooltips
- Too many modal dialogs
- State shown by color alone
- Animating live measurement values from one reading to the next (the in-between numbers were never real readings)
- Moving critical controls or changing shortcuts without telling the user
</anti_patterns>

<self_review>
Before presenting each phase, review it from every expert_panel perspective and fix what fails. End the phase with the top 3 risks, the assumptions you made, and your questions for me.
</self_review>

<output_contract>
Use Markdown with one header per phase. Use tables for inventories, specs and scorecards, ASCII wireframes for layouts, and fenced code blocks labeled with file paths. Be dense and specific; no filler.
</output_contract>

